    "q-166840": {
      "dom_id": "question-166840",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "How many cakes can the pastry shop produce with $9$ packs of chocolate?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The pastry shop can produce$\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$cakes.",
      "worked_solution": "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses $(\\frac{5x+3}{2})$ packs of chocolate. So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+3}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$\nFirst, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+3}{2} & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\ (5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5x+3 & =18 \\\\ 5x+3-3 & =18-3 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the pastry shop can produce $3$ cakes.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "",
          "tag": "mathquill",
